<header class="sg-head"><p class="featured-region">Love song · Protest and freedom · 1866 · Europe</p><h1 class="sg-title">Le Temps des cerises</h1><p class="sg-kid" data-say>A French love song from 1866 about the short season of cherries, later sung as a song of hope for a better world.</p></header>
<dl class="sg-facts"><div><dt>Words</dt><dd>Jean-Baptiste Clément</dd></div><div><dt>Music</dt><dd>Antoine Renard</dd></div><div><dt>Culture</dt><dd>French popular song; later the Paris Commune and the European left</dd></div><div><dt>Language</dt><dd>French</dd></div><div><dt>Place</dt><dd>France</dd></div><div><dt>Date</dt><dd>1866</dd></div><div><dt>Type</dt><dd>Love song · Protest and freedom</dd></div><div><dt>The words</dt><dd>Public domain (words by Jean-Baptiste Clément and music by Antoine Renard (1866))</dd></div></dl>
<div class="sg-body">
<section class="sg-sec" id="story"><h2>The story</h2><p>&quot;<a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/le-temps-des-cerises">Le Temps des cerises</a>&quot; (&quot;The Time of Cherries&quot;) is a French song written in 1866, with words by Jean-Baptiste Clément and music by Antoine Renard. It became very well known throughout the French-speaking countries.</p><p>The song came to be closely linked with the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/paris-commune">Paris Commune</a>. Verses were added to it during the Commune, and it was sung from then on as a revolutionary song: the &quot;time of cherries&quot; was read as an image of life as it would be once a revolution had changed social and economic conditions. Clément is believed to have dedicated it to a nurse who fought in the Bloody Week (semaine sanglante), when French government troops overthrew the Commune.</p><p>Many versions of the words exist. The one most often heard today is the version popularised by the singer Yves Montand; Marc Ogeret, Juliette Gréco, Cora Vaucaire and Charles Trenet are among the others who recorded it, and Bobbejaan Schoepen sang it with Geike Arnaert of Hooverphonic.</p><p>The song has remained a symbol of the left. The Communist Party of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/bohemia">Bohemia</a> and <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/moravia">Moravia</a> took two cherries into its logo because of it, the French Communist Party adopted a new logo inspired by it in 2018, and it was a symbol of The Left, a political party in Luxembourg. The town hall clock of Saint-Denis, a suburb of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/paris">Paris</a>, plays it for its hourly chime, alternating with &quot;Le roi Dagobert a mis sa culotte à l'envers&quot;. In the film Porco Rosso the character Madame Gina sings it in a cabaret.</p></section>
<section class="sg-sec" id="meaning"><h2>What it says</h2><p>On its face a love song: the brief, joyful season of cherries, when lovers go out together, is set against the lasting ache of love lost, which the singer says he will carry always. The mood is tender and wistful; after the Commune, the season of cherries was heard as a hope for a better time to come.</p></section>
<section class="sg-sec" id="words"><h2>The words</h2><div class="sg-show" role="group" aria-label="Show these columns"><button type="button" aria-pressed="true" data-col="orig">French</button><button type="button" aria-pressed="true" data-col="en">In English</button></div><div class="sg-blocks"><div class="sg-lyrics sg-cols-2"><div class="sg-colhead" aria-hidden="true"><span class="sg-o">French</span><span class="sg-e">In English</span></div><div class="sg-st"><p class="sg-ln"><span class="sg-o" lang="fr">Quand nous chanterons le temps des cerises,</span><span class="sg-e" data-say>When we sing the time of cherries,</span></p><p class="sg-ln"><span class="sg-o" lang="fr">Et gai rossignol, et merle moqueur</span><span class="sg-e" data-say>Both the gay nightingale and the mocking blackbird</span></p><p class="sg-ln"><span class="sg-o" lang="fr">Seront tous en fête !</span><span class="sg-e" data-say>Will all be in festival!</span></p><p class="sg-ln"><span class="sg-o" lang="fr">Les belles auront la folie en tête</span><span class="sg-e" data-say>The pretty girls will have madness in their heads</span></p><p class="sg-ln"><span class="sg-o" lang="fr">Et les amoureux, du soleil au cœur !</span><span class="sg-e" data-say>And the lovers, sunshine in their hearts!</span></p><p class="sg-ln"><span class="sg-o" lang="fr">Quand nous chanterons le temps des cerises,</span><span class="sg-e" data-say>When we sing the time of cherries,</span></p><p class="sg-ln"><span class="sg-o" lang="fr">Sifflera bien mieux le merle moqueur !</span><span class="sg-e" data-say>The mocking blackbird will whistle much better!</span></p></div><div class="sg-st"><p class="sg-ln"><span class="sg-o" lang="fr">Mais il est bien court, le temps des cerises</span><span class="sg-e" data-say>But it is very short, the time of cherries</span></p><p class="sg-ln"><span class="sg-o" lang="fr">Où l’on s’en va deux, cueillir en rêvant</span><span class="sg-e" data-say>When we go off in twos, to pick while dreaming</span></p><p class="sg-ln"><span class="sg-o" lang="fr">Des pendants d’oreilles…</span><span class="sg-e" data-say>Earrings...</span></p><p class="sg-ln"><span class="sg-o" lang="fr">Cerises d’amour aux roses pareilles,</span><span class="sg-e" data-say>Cherries of love like roses,</span></p><p class="sg-ln"><span class="sg-o" lang="fr">Tombant sous la feuille en gouttes de sang…</span><span class="sg-e" data-say>Falling under the leaves in drops of blood...</span></p><p class="sg-ln"><span class="sg-o" lang="fr">Mais il est bien court, le temps des cerises,</span><span class="sg-e" data-say>But it is very short, the time of cherries,</span></p><p class="sg-ln"><span class="sg-o" lang="fr">Pendants de corail qu’on cueille en rêvant !</span><span class="sg-e" data-say>Pendants of coral that one picks while dreaming!</span></p></div><div class="sg-st"><p class="sg-ln"><span class="sg-o" lang="fr">Quand vous en serez au temps des cerises,</span><span class="sg-e" data-say>When you come to the time of cherries,</span></p><p class="sg-ln"><span class="sg-o" lang="fr">Si vous avez peur des chagrins d’amour,</span><span class="sg-e" data-say>If you are afraid of the sorrows of love,</span></p><p class="sg-ln"><span class="sg-o" lang="fr">Évitez les belles !</span><span class="sg-e" data-say>Avoid the pretty girls!</span></p><p class="sg-ln"><span class="sg-o" lang="fr">Moi qui ne crains pas les peines cruelles,</span><span class="sg-e" data-say>I who do not fear cruel pains,</span></p><p class="sg-ln"><span class="sg-o" lang="fr">Je ne vivrai point sans souffrir un jour…</span><span class="sg-e" data-say>I will not live a single day without suffering...</span></p><p class="sg-ln"><span class="sg-o" lang="fr">Quand vous en serez au temps des cerises,</span><span class="sg-e" data-say>When you come to the time of cherries,</span></p><p class="sg-ln"><span class="sg-o" lang="fr">Vous aurez aussi des peines d’amour !</span><span class="sg-e" data-say>You will have the pains of love too!</span></p></div><div class="sg-st"><p class="sg-ln"><span class="sg-o" lang="fr">J’aimerai toujours le temps des cerises :</span><span class="sg-e" data-say>I will always love the time of cherries:</span></p><p class="sg-ln"><span class="sg-o" lang="fr">C’est de ce temps-là que je garde au cœur</span><span class="sg-e" data-say>It is from that time that I keep in my heart</span></p><p class="sg-ln"><span class="sg-o" lang="fr">Une plaie ouverte !</span><span class="sg-e" data-say>An open wound!</span></p><p class="sg-ln"><span class="sg-o" lang="fr">Et dame Fortune, en m’étant offerte,</span><span class="sg-e" data-say>And Lady Fortune, though she offered herself to me,</span></p><p class="sg-ln"><span class="sg-o" lang="fr">Ne pourra jamais fermer ma douleur…</span><span class="sg-e" data-say>Will never be able to close my pain...</span></p><p class="sg-ln"><span class="sg-o" lang="fr">J’aimerai toujours le temps des cerises</span><span class="sg-e" data-say>I will always love the time of cherries</span></p><p class="sg-ln"><span class="sg-o" lang="fr">Et le souvenir que je garde au cœur !</span><span class="sg-e" data-say>And the memory that I keep in my heart!</span></p></div></div></div><div class="sg-credit"><p>Original: Jean-Baptiste Clément (1866). Text from <a href="https://fr.wikisource.org/wiki/Le_Temps_des_cerises" target="_blank" rel="noopener">Wikisource</a>; public domain.</p><p>The English is the Institute&#8217;s own translation, made from the original for this page.</p></div><details class="sg-notes"><summary>Notes on the translation (3)</summary><ul><li>line 2.3: 'pendants d'oreilles', earrings: pairs of cherries hung over the ears.</li><li>line 3.4-3.5: literally 'I who do not fear cruel pains, I shall not live without suffering one day'; read either as 'not a day without suffering' or 'I shall not go without suffering some day'.</li><li>line 4.4: 'en m'étant offerte', literally 'in being offered to me': even if Fortune were offered to me.</li></ul></details></section>
</div>
<section class="sg-sec" id="listen"><h2>Listen</h2><div class="sg-video" data-yt="V190BbP_LBk" data-title="Le Temps des cerises: Yves Montand, on French television (RTF, 1958; the INA archive)"><button type="button" class="sg-play"><span class="sg-play-ic" aria-hidden="true"></span><span class="sg-play-t">Play the performance</span><span class="sg-play-n">Yves Montand, on French television (RTF, 1958; the INA archive)</span></button></div><p class="sg-video-cr">On YouTube, from the channel INA Chansons. Pressing play loads YouTube&#8217;s player. <a href="https://www.youtube.com/watch?v=V190BbP_LBk" target="_blank" rel="noopener">Watch on YouTube<span aria-hidden="true"> &#8599;</span></a></p></section>
<section class="sg-sec" id="inspired"><h2>What it inspired</h2><ul class="sg-chain"><li><span class="sg-chain-n">Logo of the Communist Party of Bohemia and Moravia</span><span class="sg-chain-h">two cherries taken into the party's logo</span></li><li><span class="sg-chain-n">Logo of the French Communist Party</span> <span class="sg-chain-y">2018</span><span class="sg-chain-h">new logo inspired by the song</span></li><li><span class="sg-chain-n">Porco Rosso (film)</span><span class="sg-chain-h">sung in a cabaret scene by Madame Gina</span></li><li><span class="sg-chain-n">Saint-Denis town hall clock chime</span><span class="sg-chain-h">used as the hourly chime</span></li></ul></section>
